<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Regino of Prüm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 915</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Regino of Prüm was a Frankish monk, abbot of Prüm from 892 to 899 and later head of St. Martin's Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, where he died in 915. His Chronicle, a world history running to 906, is a major source for the late Carolingian period. He also wrote a handbook of church discipline for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> visiting their dioceses and a treatise on music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/regino-of-prum.jpg" alt="Regino of Prüm" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas de Larmessin (I) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regino_von_Pr%C3%BCm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to statements of a later era, Regino was the son of noble parents and was born at the stronghold of Altrip on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near Speyer, at an unknown date. He entered the Benedictine Order, probably at Prüm itself, and was a diligent student. The Imperial Abbey of Prüm suffered greatly from Norse raids in the 9th century and was twice seized and ravaged, in 882 and 892. After the second devastation the abbot Farabert resigned and Regino was elected in 892. His work of restoration was hampered by the struggle between contending parties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><p>In 899 Richarius, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Liège, drove Regino from office and became abbot himself. Regino went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, where Archbishop Ratbod received him honourably and made him abbot of St Martin's, which he reformed and rebuilt after Norse devastation. He supported the archbishop's church reforms and accompanied him on visitations. He died at Trier in 915 and was buried in St Maximin's Abbey, where his tomb was found in 1581.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His earliest work was a treatise on music, the Epistola de harmonica institutione, written as a letter to Ratbod to improve liturgical singing in the diocese. He also compiled the Tonarius, a collection of chants. At Ratbod's request he drew up a collection of canons for use in visitations, divided into 434 sections and dedicated to Hatto I, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. Large parts of it were later included in the Decretum of Burchard of 1012. Its section 364 is the Canon Episcopi, which deals with popular superstition.</p><p>His most influential work is the Chronicon, a universal history from the Incarnation to 906 that centres on the Carolingian empire and links the rise and fall of the dynasty with his own affairs. Its first book runs to the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> in 741 and draws on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, Paulus Diaconus and the Annals of Saint-Amand. The second book begins with a long excerpt of the Royal Frankish Annals down to 813, and later sections criticise Zwentibald, son of Arnulf, in the contest for Lotharingia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Regino changed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s Anno Mundi dating to one counted from the Incarnation, and modern scholars consider the Chronicon the first chronicle to apply that system conventionally, although its chronological accuracy has been questioned. It is the only contemporary text that hints at the organisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-preslav">Council of Preslav</a>. The Chronicon was continued to 967 and was used by later writers such as Cosmas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. It was first printed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in 1521, and Simon MacLean published an English edition in 2009. Around 900 Regino also named four marks of a people: descent, custom, language and law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Regino of Prüm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abbot <b>Regino of Prüm</b> wrote an important chronicle there, and Emperor Lothair I retired to the abbey and died there in 855.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-prum">Imperial Abbey of Prüm</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Altrip, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.43,8.5&amp;year=905" aria-label="Altrip on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Regino of Prüm"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5471 5868h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5471 5868h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.43,8.5&amp;year=905">What was here in 905 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
